WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:03.092 --> 00:00:04.012
Overview

2
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.834
In short: Matt LeMay shatters
the myth of the "hero" PM:

3
00:00:08.834 --> 00:00:12.364
the role isn't about a
brilliant vision but about the

4
00:00:12.364 --> 00:00:16.409
daily connective work between
people, teams, and goals.

5
00:00:16.723 --> 00:00:20.803
Most product management books
describe the job as it should look:

6
00:00:20.803 --> 00:00:24.436
brilliant strategy, aligned
teams, data-driven decisions.

7
00:00:24.436 --> 00:00:27.239
Matt LeMay wrote the opposite book.

8
00:00:27.537 --> 00:00:31.730
Product Management in Practice
describes the job as it actually is:

9
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.475
meetings that go sideways,
executives who change direction,

10
00:00:35.475 --> 00:00:37.935
information that is never complete —

11
00:00:37.935 --> 00:00:42.137
and organizational politics that
no theory prepares you for.

12
00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:46.046
Not another book about how product
management is supposed to work,

13
00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:49.340
but a survival guide for what
really happens on the ground.

14
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.903
The Author, and Why the Book Exists.

15
00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:56.507
LeMay is a veteran product consultant who
has worked with companies like Spotify,

16
00:00:56.507 --> 00:00:58.403
Google, and Prudential.

17
00:00:58.776 --> 00:01:02.754
After coaching dozens of teams,
he kept seeing the same pattern:

18
00:01:02.754 --> 00:01:05.571
new PMs show up armed with perfect theory —

19
00:01:05.571 --> 00:01:09.949
and break against a chaotic reality
that appears in no syllabus.

20
00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:15.796
The book, first published in 2017 and
updated in a second edition in 2022,

21
00:01:15.796 --> 00:01:19.099
was written precisely for that gap.

22
00:01:19.497 --> 00:01:21.138
Right in the subtitle,

23
00:01:21.138 --> 00:01:25.351
LeMay defines product management as
a fundamentally connective role —

24
00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:28.852
a profession measured by your
ability to connect people,

25
00:01:28.852 --> 00:01:32.207
teams, and ideas, not by technical brilliance.

26
00:01:32.503 --> 00:01:35.151
The Pragmatic Approach: Start with the Work.

27
00:01:35.151 --> 00:01:37.188
The book's organizing principle:

28
00:01:37.188 --> 00:01:41.254
do not start from a framework and
try to bend reality to fit it.

29
00:01:41.582 --> 00:01:45.061
Start from the challenge on the
table, understand it deeply,

30
00:01:45.061 --> 00:01:47.916
and only then reach for a suitable tool.

31
00:01:48.219 --> 00:01:52.654
According to LeMay, the most common PM
problem is not a shortage of tools —

32
00:01:52.654 --> 00:01:56.366
it is a shortage of understanding
of the real problem.

33
00:01:56.653 --> 00:02:01.775
Along the way, the book shatters a few
foundational illusions about the role:

34
00:02:01.775 --> 00:02:04.948
The illusion · The reality, per LeMay.

35
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.140
"The PM is a mini-CEO" ·
Enormous responsibility,

36
00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:14.551
near-zero formal authority — you
lead through trust and influence.

37
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.246
"There is one standard job description" ·

38
00:02:17.246 --> 00:02:19.996
The role looks different
in every organization —

39
00:02:19.996 --> 00:02:22.753
you redefine it through the work itself.

40
00:02:23.131 --> 00:02:24.599
"That is not my job" ·

41
00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:28.757
If it matters to the product and nobody
is doing it — it is exactly your job.

42
00:02:28.757 --> 00:02:30.477
Who Should Read It.

43
00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:35.426
The book's power is its honesty:
LeMay does not sell a magic method;

44
00:02:35.426 --> 00:02:39.245
he offers uncomfortable truths,
field-tested scenarios,

45
00:02:39.245 --> 00:02:42.494
and guiding questions in every chapter.

46
00:02:42.837 --> 00:02:46.933
For a PM at the start of the road it
is an onboarding guide; for a veteran,

47
00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:51.873
a mirror that explains why things stall even
when everything is done "by the book. "

48
00:02:52.217 --> 00:02:57.096
Velox in action: Michal, the product
manager of Velox (a task-management app),

49
00:02:57.096 --> 00:03:00.047
got an "urgent" demand from the VP of Sales:

50
00:03:00.047 --> 00:03:03.227
a new dashboard for a strategic customer.

51
00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.595
Instead of sprinting to
a prioritization matrix,

52
00:03:06.595 --> 00:03:10.372
she followed LeMay's advice and
started with the work itself:

53
00:03:10.372 --> 00:03:15.080
two clarifying calls revealed the customer
did not need a new feature at all —

54
00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:19.476
he simply did not know the existing
report could filter by teams.

55
00:03:19.825 --> 00:03:23.508
One hour of listening saved a
month of development. Bottom Line.

56
00:03:23.508 --> 00:03:26.525
This is not the book that
tells you what to build —

57
00:03:26.525 --> 00:03:29.227
strategy and discovery books do that.

58
00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:34.272
This is the book that teaches you how
to operate inside the real day-to-day:

59
00:03:34.272 --> 00:03:36.506
ambiguity, people, and politics.

60
00:03:36.506 --> 00:03:38.741
Heading into your first PM role?

61
00:03:38.741 --> 00:03:40.873
Read it before day one.

62
00:03:41.183 --> 00:03:42.923
Already deep in the mess?

63
00:03:42.923 --> 00:03:47.352
It will explain why it feels this way
— and what to do about it. Matt LeMay.

64
00:03:47.352 --> 00:03:50.336
Author of Product Management in Practice.

65
00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:55.724
"The magic of PM is in the daily
connective work — not in the vision. "

66
00:03:56.018 --> 00:03:59.328
The PM is the puzzle piece
that connects all the rest —

67
00:03:59.328 --> 00:04:02.986
no glamour, but without it
the picture isn't complete.
